    "narrative": "The LawDog starting lineup for July 31 is anchored by two high-ceiling anchors: **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (84.27) and **Bryce Harper** (80.21). **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a significant SP matchup against Bratt, whose 6.32 xERA is the softest landing spot on our board today. Meanwhile, **Bryce Harper** remains an elite start, leveraging a strong park factor and a clean matchup against Young to maintain his high projection. **Dylan Beavers** (69.52) and **Jonathan Aranda** (68.94) round out our top-tier plays, both thriving on favorable SP skill matchups and beneficial hitting environments that effectively maximize their efficiency scores despite the premium on stolen bases.\n\nWe are capitalizing on aggressive platoon and park advantages today. **Gleyber Torres** (67.46) finds himself in an ideal spot against Springs, with his 13% platoon boost and a positive park adjustment driving his strong score. Similarly, **Taylor Ward** (68.06) and **Tyler Freeman** (68.78) are maximizing their 16% and 14% park boosts respectively. The most difficult decision this morning was sidelining **Kyle Karros**, who posted a respectable 59.60. Ultimately, **Kyle Karros** was pushed to the bench because the cumulative efficiency of our outfield and infield starters\u2014specifically the speed-centric profiles of **Daulton Varsho** (62.50) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (63.00)\u2014provides a higher probability of capturing the scarce SB category required for our roto standing.\n\nWe must remain cautious with the \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Cal Raleigh**, **Gleyber Torres**, and **Lawrence Butler**. While their projections assume historical batting order positions, any late-scratch or shift toward the bottom of the order could deflate their real-world output. **Yainer Diaz** (42.44) is our most vulnerable starter, hovering near the marginal threshold; facing Eovaldi\u2019s 3.37 xERA provides little margin for error. We will monitor the weather and official lineups closely to ensure our daily game caps are not wasted on underperforming slots.",
